  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 736 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nThe axis of an upper level trough is shown to be right over the \nSoutheast to start the long term, with shortwaves rippling along the \nflow across the ArkLaMiss and into the Tennessee Valley. This trough \nwill gradually be pushed northeast as an upper ridge over the Desert \nSouthwest builds over the southern Plains and eastward into the \nSoutheast this weekend. At the surface, a boundary looks to linger\nover central Alabama, Mississippi, and Georgia through midweek; \nbut, another cold front is expected to push through the Tennessee \nValley on Thursday. There may be a reinforcing front that swings \nthrough on Friday, but high pressure is shown to quickly build \nover the Ohio Valley, Appalachians, and portions of the Northeast \nnext weekend. Overall, the best chance of showers and storms will \nlikely be Wednesday night through Thursday. Chances then decrease \nfrom late week through the weekend as drier air is filtered into \nthe region.\n\nGuidance indicates that PWATs increase to around 2 inches late \nWednesday through Thursday. While there will likely be sufficient \ninstability with highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s Thursday \nafternoon, model bulk shear values and lapse rates have decreased \nsince yesterday. So, confidence in stronger storms (downbursts) has \nalso decreased; however, these trends will continue to be monitored. \nRegardless, showers and storms will be efficient rainfall producers. \nHeavy rain, especially from cells moving over the same locations \nrepeatedly, may result in at least minor flooding issues. Ultimately,\nkeep any eye on forecast updates this week and turn around, don't \ndrown if you encounter flooded roads! For Friday through Sunday, rain\nchances are lower with continued high temperatures in the upper 80s \nto lower 90s and lows in the 60s.",
